---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import Tune from "../components/Tune.astro";

// Charity not chosen yet. Set these once the family decides.
const CHARITY = { name: "", url: "" };
// The photo-essay version of this page (full-bleed photos, scroll scenes) is kept in src/drafts/photo-essay.astro.
---
<Base title="Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns, 1929 to 2026">
  <Nav current="obituary" />
  <Tune />

  <header class="cover plain">
    <div class="cover-title col">
      <span class="lady" data-reveal>Lady</span>
      <h1 data-split>Margaret</h1>
      <span class="fullname" data-reveal>Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns · 1929 – 2026</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <div class="col prose">
      <p class="first">Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns, who left the poverty of postwar Glasgow at nineteen and remade herself in Montreal as a model, a mother of four, and, in the eyes of her family, a lady, died peacefully there on November 5, 2026, surrounded by family. She was 97.</p>
      <p>Some people pass through life quietly. Margaret was not one of them.</p>
      <p class="dropcap">Born in Glasgow in 1929, Margaret was one of seven children raised in the Garngad, one of the poorest districts in the city. Her father, Hugh Cairns, struggled with alcoholism and was in and out of prison; her mother, Helen Kennedy, died suddenly when Margaret was only six. Then came the war, and German bombs over Glasgow. Left largely to fend for themselves, the Kennedy-Cairns children relied on one another and did whatever it took to keep fed, in circumstances that asked far more of them than their years.</p>
      <p>Yet it was there that the qualities that would carry her through life took root: resourcefulness, resilience, a fierce self-sufficiency, and an unshakeable instinct to provide for those who depended on her.</p>
      <p>She came, too, from a line of remarkable, and remarkably headstrong, women. Her maternal grandmother, Helen Orr Kennedy, rose to become a senior leader—a Worthy Mistress—in Scotland’s women’s Orange Order. The staunchly Protestant Order forbade marriage to Catholics, so when her daughter Helen wed Hugh Cairns, an Irish Catholic immigrant, against the wishes of both families, it was a genuine scandal. The two sides kept their distance from then on, but Margaret would not. After her mother died, she ran away again and again to her grandmother’s house, a modest, respectable home that, after the tenements of the Garngad, must have seemed palatial. It was an early sign of a girl who would go wherever she believed a better life lay, whatever lines stood in her way.</p>
      <p>In her late teens, Margaret was studying to become a nun. For a motherless girl from the Garngad, the convent offered what little else did: an education, security, and a respectable way out. But it could never have held a spirit as restless and bright as hers. At nineteen she left the convent and instead crossed the Atlantic to begin a new life in Montreal with her fiancé, Henry, Canadian-born but also of Irish and Scottish parents, whom she had known only briefly.</p>
      <p>It left an ocean between her and the brothers and sisters with whom she had survived her childhood.</p>
      <p>Throughout her twenties, she bore and raised three children within that first marriage, isolated in a new country, far from anyone of her own. It is hard to imagine how lonely those years must have been. Her husband suffered from a brain injury and was, by every account, a difficult man. Despite her young age, it was a burden she knew all too well; she had grown up in the shadow of a troubled father and now found herself living with one again. In the late 1950s she divorced him, a step that took real nerve for any woman of the time, and more still for one raised Catholic.</p>
      <p>The years that followed were the hardest of her adult life. Not yet thirty, divorced, and with three young children to feed, Margaret worked as a clerk by day and cleaned offices by night. She was determined that her children would never know the poverty she had, and though it stretched her means to the limit, she rented in Westmount, one of the city’s most exclusive neighbourhoods, for the sake of its schools. Along the way she had stints as a model, and it was easy to see why: she had striking Scottish looks, with vivid red hair, expressive features, and a smile that could light up a room.</p>
      <p>At the turn of the 1960s she married Ted Liontos, a Greek photographer with dashing, James Bond good looks, and in May 1961 their daughter, Anthea, was born, Margaret’s youngest child. The marriage lasted only a few years, and before long she was once again raising her children on her own, now four of them.</p>
      <p>Through the 1960s and 1970s she worked tirelessly to provide for them, often behind the counters of Montreal’s finest stores, among them Birks, the storied jeweller, where her magnetism made her a natural. Suitors were never in short supply, but any man who wanted Margaret without her children was soon shown the door; they came together or not at all. With Ted’s help, Anthea went on to a private school, and in time became the first in the family to go to university, at McGill.</p>
      <p>Remarkably for a single mother of the time, Margaret eventually bought a house of her own in Westmount. She became a familiar face in the city’s anglophone society, holding her own among its best-connected families with an ease few would have traced back to a Glasgow tenement. None of her children’s friends ever guessed how close to the bone the family was living.</p>
      <p>She was glamorous, opinionated, endlessly sociable, and blessed with a personality that could fill a room before she’d properly entered it. She loved beautiful things, good conversation, and the pleasure of making an impression. She married four times. She was quick to laugh, occasionally quicker to anger, and possessed of a Scottish wit as sharp as her spectacularly stubborn opinions. She loved a practical joke, never more than at Christmas, when a little Tiffany box in a stocking might turn out to hold a set of false teeth or a few toenail clippings.</p>
      <p>She had a gift for turning an ordinary encounter into an occasion. Left on her own for a few hours on the Amalfi Coast, she came back the best of friends with a countess she had only just met. And she was rarely far from a song. She had a beautiful singing voice, part of a musical family that also gave the world her niece, the Scottish singer Lulu, and it filled the family home.</p>
      <p>Those years brought a change of faith as well. In the 1970s, amid the Westmount circles where it had a loyal following, Margaret discovered Christian Science. Born into a religious divide she had never chosen, she embraced it entirely on her own terms, becoming a devoted student of Mary Baker Eddy and her teaching that spiritual reality, not material circumstance, is the truth of a person. For a woman whose life had been shaped by hardship, the idea that her circumstances need not define her held an obvious appeal. She was rarely shy about it, and a conversation with Margaret, whether with a grandson, a neighbour, or a nurse she had known for ten minutes, could soon turn to the healing power of right thinking.</p>
      <p>In the early 1980s she lost her son Colin and her daughter Helen, both in their twenties, within thirteen months of one another. No strength or determination could spare her that unspeakable grief. She carried it for the rest of her life, and somehow kept her appetite for beauty, laughter, and the company of others.</p>
      <p>When her daughter Anthea was later raising a young son on her own while working in Manhattan, Margaret would not hear of leaving him to strangers. Decades after crossing an ocean in search of a future for herself, she left her beloved Montreal for New York City to help build one for her grandson, Sean, and helped raise him herself.</p>
      <p>In later life, her son Paul gifted her a small plot of land in her native Scotland, along with a certificate naming her Lady Margaret. She was touched by the thought but never entirely comfortable with the title, which struck her as faintly fraudulent. The irony was lost on no one else. She had become more of a lady than most people will ever meet, and she had needed no certificate to do it.</p>
      <p>In her final years, age took from her the self-sufficiency she had always prized. For a woman who had so often been the one others depended upon, learning to depend on them was no small thing. Her family stayed close, grateful for the chance to return some of the care she had given so freely. And she was still singing in her very last days.</p>
      <p>She had crossed an ocean, raised four children, lost two of them, and made and remade a life in a city far from the one where she was born. She had known poverty and elegance, loneliness and great affection, heartbreak and laughter. Through it all she remained a devoted mother and nana, a woman of faith, and an irrepressible force of nature.</p>
      <p>She was predeceased by her children Colin and Helen; her last partner, George Kendall; and her six brothers and sisters, Nellie, Betty, James, Davey, Hugh Jr., and Colin. She is survived by her son, Paul; her daughter, Anthea; and her grandson, Sean, who will carry her memory forward with love, laughter, and gratitude.</p>
      <p>In accordance with her wishes, Margaret will be cremated following a private family ceremony, and her ashes scattered where she will join Colin and Helen once more.</p>
      <p>It will come as no surprise that, even in her final days, her wit was as sharp as ever. When nurses explained that they needed to take medical images of her ribs, the former model asked, without missing a beat, “Am I being paid for these pictures?”</p>
    </div>

    <div class="col prose to-book">
      <p><a href="/memories">Share a memory of Margaret, or read what others have written, in the memory book.</a></p>
    </div>

    {CHARITY.name && (
      <section class="col prose tribute">
        <p>In lieu of flowers, the family asks that you consider a donation to {CHARITY.name}, a cause close to Margaret’s heart.</p>
        <a class="btn" href={CHARITY.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Donate to {CHARITY.name}</a>
      </section>
    )}
  </main>

  <footer class="col">
    <p>Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns · Glasgow 1929 · Montreal 2026</p>
  </footer>

  <script>
    import "../scripts/motion.ts";
  </script>
</Base>
